// ---------- MINI-LESSONS (one card per key section) ----------
window.LESSONS = {
  0: [
    { s: "1. What good is logic?", b: `<p>Kreeft gives thirteen reasons, beginning with <b>order</b> (logic builds the habit of thinking in an orderly way) and <b>power</b> (the power of proof, which can be used to win the truth or merely to win the argument).</p><p>Others: better <b>reading</b> and <b>writing</b>, <b>happiness</b>, <b>religious faith</b>, <b>wisdom</b>, <b>democracy</b>, knowing logic's own <b>limits</b>, recognizing <b>contradictions</b>, <b>certainty</b>, and <b>truth</b>.</p>` },
    { s: "3. The two logics (P)", b: `<p>For 2,300 years students learned <b>Aristotelian logic</b> (Aristotle's <i>Organon</i>, "instrument"). Since Russell and Whitehead's <i>Principia Mathematica</i> (1913), most textbooks teach <b>symbolic (mathematical) logic</b>.</p><p>Kreeft compares symbolic logic to shorthand: efficient for long, complex arguments. Aristotelian logic is "longhand": closer to ordinary language, better for beginners, and it keeps the notions of <b>essence</b> and <b>nature</b> that philosophy needs.</p>` },
    { s: "4. All of logic in two pages (B)", b: `<p>Reasoning moves from <b>premises</b> (reasons) to a <b>conclusion</b> (what you try to prove). <b>Induction</b> reasons from particulars and gives only probability; <b>deduction</b> reasons from at least one universal premise and, when correct, gives certainty.</p><p>A deductive argument proves its conclusion only if it passes <b>three checkpoints</b>:</p><ul><li>All <b>terms</b> are clear and unambiguous.</li><li>All <b>premises</b> are true.</li><li>The argument is logically <b>valid</b>.</li></ul><p>So to disagree with a conclusion you must find an ambiguous term, a false premise, or a logical fallacy. If you can't, honesty demands you admit it has been proved.</p>` },
    { s: "5. The three acts of the mind (B)", b: `<table class="tbl"><tr><th></th><th>1st act</th><th>2nd act</th><th>3rd act</th></tr><tr><td>Act</td><td>Under&shy;standing (simple apprehension)</td><td>Judging</td><td>Reasoning</td></tr><tr><td>Product</td><td>Concept</td><td>Judgment</td><td>Argument</td></tr><tr><td>In logic</td><td>Term</td><td>Proposition</td><td>Argument (usually a syllogism)</td></tr><tr><td>In language</td><td>Word or phrase</td><td>Declarative sentence</td><td>Paragraph</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>None</td><td>Subject & predicate</td><td>Premises & conclusion</td></tr><tr><td>Answers</td><td>What it is</td><td>Whether it is</td><td>Why it is</td></tr><tr><td>Reveals</td><td>Essence</td><td>Existence</td><td>Cause</td></tr><tr><td>Good when</td><td>Clear</td><td>True</td><td>Valid</td></tr><tr><td>Ask</td><td>What do you mean?</td><td>What's your point?</td><td>Why? Prove it.</td></tr></table><p>Only terms are clear or unclear; only propositions are true or false; only arguments are valid or invalid.</p>` },
  ],
  1: [
    { s: "1. Understanding (P)", b: `<p>What distinguishes us from both beasts and computers is <b>understanding</b>, shown in our ability to <b>ask questions</b>. Computers never question their programming; animals can't formulate questions.</p><p>Concepts, the products of understanding, are <b>abstract</b> and <b>universal</b>; relations between concepts are <b>necessary</b>; and concepts are <b>unchanging</b>. Their most important feature is universality: one meaning true of many things ("tree" applies to every tree).</p>` },
    { s: "2. Concepts, terms and words (P)", b: `<p>A <b>concept</b> is in an individual mind. A <b>term</b> expresses objectively what is known. A <b>word</b> is the physical, conventional, changeable expression of a term in a particular language ("love," <i>caritas</i>, <i>agape</i>, <i>amour</i> are one term in several words).</p><p>A term is any word or group of words that can be the subject or predicate of a proposition (Latin <i>terminus</i>, "end"). Terms are never true or false, only clear or unclear.</p>` },
    { s: "3. The \"problem of universals\" (P)", b: `<p>What in reality do universal terms like "humanity" refer to?</p><ul><li><b>Extreme Realism</b> (Plato): universals ("Forms") are real things in another world.</li><li><b>Nominalism</b> (Ockham): universals are only names. Kreeft argues this contradicts itself: calling all trees "trees" presupposes they share something.</li><li><b>Moderate Realism</b> (Aristotle, Avicenna, Aquinas): essences are objectively real, but they exist as universal only in the mind, by abstraction, and in things only individually.</li></ul>` },
    { s: "4. Extension and comprehension", b: `<p>The <b>extension</b> of a term is all the things it refers to (its "population"). Its <b>comprehension</b> is its inner meaning (man = "rational animal").</p><p>Synonym pairs: comprehension/<b>intension</b>/connotation/meaning and extension/<b>denotation</b>/reference. Extension is quantitative; comprehension is qualitative.</p><p><b>Principle of Inverse Relation:</b> as comprehension increases, extension decreases. "Animal" has more extension but less comprehension than "man."</p><p>Divisions analyze extension; definitions analyze comprehension.</p>` },
  ],
  2: [
    { s: "1. Classifying terms", b: `<p>Kreeft gives twelve ways terms can be classified. Terms are either:</p><ul><li>unambiguous or ambiguous; clear or unclear; exact or vague</li><li><b>univocal</b> (one meaning), <b>equivocal</b> (unrelated meanings), or <b>analogical</b> (related meanings, partly same and partly different)</li><li>literal or metaphorical (a metaphor is literally false; an analogy is not)</li><li>positive or negative; simple or complex; categorematic or syncategorematic</li><li><b>universal, particular, or singular</b></li><li><b>collective</b> (the group as a whole) or <b>divisive</b> (each member)</li><li>concrete ("red") or abstract ("redness"); absolute ("man") or relative ("father")</li></ul>` },
    { s: "2. Categories (B)", b: `<p>Aristotle's ten <b>categories</b>, the <i>summa genera</i> (highest classes):</p><ol><li>substance</li><li>quantity</li><li>quality</li><li>relation</li><li>place</li><li>time</li><li>posture</li><li>possession</li><li>action</li><li>passion (being acted upon)</li></ol><p>Parts of speech roughly correspond: nouns express substances, adjectives qualities or quantities, prepositions relations, verbs actions or passions.</p>` },
    { s: "3. Predicables (B)", b: `<p>The five <b>predicables</b> classify how a predicate relates to its subject, by how close it comes to stating the subject's essence:</p><ul><li><b>Species</b>: the whole essence ("Man is a rational animal")</li><li><b>Genus</b>: the common part of the essence ("animal")</li><li><b>Specific difference</b>: the distinguishing part ("rational")</li><li><b>Property</b>: not the essence but flows from it, always present ("able to laugh," "mortal")</li><li><b>Accident</b>: can come and go ("bald," "Athenian")</li></ul><p>Categories classify terms in themselves; predicables classify predicates relative to subjects. In comprehension a genus is part of a species; in extension a species is part of a genus. The <b>Tree of Porphyry</b> maps the genera from Being down to man.</p>` },
    { s: "4. Division and outlining (B)", b: `<p>Dividing a term divides its <b>extension</b>. Three rules:</p><ol><li><b>Exclusive</b>: the parts must not overlap.</li><li><b>Exhaustive</b>: the parts must add up to the whole.</li><li><b>One standard</b>: use only one basis of division.</li></ol><p>A <b>dichotomous</b> division ("loving / non-loving") automatically obeys rule 1. Outlining is the most practical application, and the first rule of outlining is: just do it! No "A" without a "B."</p>` },
  ],
  3: [
    { s: "Material vs. formal fallacies", b: `<p><b>Formal</b> fallacies are errors in reasoning, the third act of the mind (covered later). <b>Material</b> fallacies are errors in the meaning or content of terms, the first act of the mind.</p><p>Kreeft lists <b>49 material fallacies in 7 groups of 7</b>, the most common placed first in each group. Fallacies of language, diversion, oversimplification, argumentation, induction, procedure, and metaphysics.</p>` },
    { s: "1. Fallacies of language", b: `<ul><li><b>Equivocation</b>: a term shifts meaning in the argument. "To unmake an ambiguity, make a distinction."</li><li><b>Amphiboly</b>: ambiguous grammar or syntax</li><li><b>Accent</b>: ambiguity from tone, emphasis, or innuendo</li><li><b>Slanting</b>: the "question-begging epithet"; a word that evaluates while it describes</li><li><b>Slogans</b> used as substitutes for argument</li><li><b>Hyperbole</b>: exaggeration</li><li><b>"Straw Man"</b>: refuting a weak caricature of the opponent's view</li></ul>` },
    { s: "2. Fallacies of diversion", b: `<ul><li><b>Ad hominem</b>: attacking the person (including Poisoning the Well, <i>Tu quoque</i>, and the Genetic Fallacy)</li><li><b>Ad verecundiam</b>: appeal to illegitimate authority</li><li><b>Ad baculum</b>: appeal to force</li><li><b>Ad misericordiam</b>: appeal to pity</li><li><b>Ad ignominiam</b>: appeal to shame</li><li><b>Ad populum</b>: appeal to the masses (flattery, "everybody does it," "the polls say," snob appeal, "the Big Lie"…)</li><li><b>Ad ignorantiam</b>: "it must be true since we don't know it isn't"</li></ul>` },
    { s: "3. Fallacies of oversimplification", b: `<ul><li><b>Dicto simpliciter</b>: applying a general principle to a special case without the needed qualification</li><li><b>Special Case</b>: the reverse; arguing from a special case to the general rule</li><li><b>Composition</b>: from part to whole</li><li><b>Division</b>: from whole to part</li><li><b>The Black-and-White Fallacy</b>: allowing no gradations between extremes</li><li><b>Quoting out of Context</b></li><li><b>Stereotyping</b></li></ul>` },
    { s: "4. Fallacies of argumentation", b: `<ul><li><b>Non sequitur</b>: "it does not follow" (the master fallacy of this group)</li><li><b>Ignoratio elenchi</b>: irrelevant conclusion, "missing the point"</li><li><b>Petitio principii</b>: begging the question, assuming what is to be proved</li><li><b>Complex Question</b>: "Have you stopped beating your wife?"</li><li><b>Arguing in a Circle</b>: using the conclusion to prove the premise</li><li><b>Contradictory Premises</b></li><li><b>False Assumption</b></li></ul>` },
    { s: "5. Inductive fallacies", b: `<ul><li><b>Hasty Generalization</b></li><li><b>Post hoc ergo propter hoc</b>: "after this, therefore because of this"</li><li><b>Hypothesis Contrary to Fact</b>: the "if only" fallacy</li><li><b>False Analogy</b></li><li><b>Argument from Silence</b></li><li><b>Selective Evidence</b></li><li><b>Slanting the Question</b> (as in polls)</li></ul>` },
    { s: "6. Procedural fallacies", b: `<ul><li>"Refuting" an argument by refuting its conclusion</li><li>Assuming that refuting an argument disproves its conclusion</li><li>Ignoring the argument (arguing "beside the point")</li><li>Substituting explanations for proofs</li><li>Answering another argument than the one given</li><li>Shifting the burden of proof</li><li>Winning the argument but losing the arguer, or vice versa</li></ul>` },
    { s: "7. Metaphysical fallacies", b: `<ul><li><b>Reductionism</b> or "Nothing Buttery" ("love is nothing but lust")</li><li><b>The Fallacy of Accident</b>: confusing the accidental with the essential</li><li><b>Confusing Quantity with Quality</b></li><li><b>Misplaced Concreteness</b>: treating an abstraction as a concrete thing</li><li>Confusing the logical, physical, and psychological "because"</li><li><b>The Existential Fallacy</b>: confusing essence and existence</li><li><b>Confusing the Natural with the Common</b></li></ul><p>Then enjoy Max Shulman's story "Love Is a Fallacy," where Polly turns the fallacies back on her teacher.</p>` },
  ],
  4: [
    { s: "1. The nature of definition (B)", b: `<p>A definition tells us <b>what a thing is</b>; it heals the disease of ambiguity. Kreeft calls this the most important chapter of the first third of the book.</p><p>Definition analyzes a term's <b>comprehension</b> (as division analyzes its extension). The ideal is an <b>essential definition</b>: <b>genus + specific difference</b>. The minimum is that it distinguish the thing from everything else ("definition" from <i>de-finio</i>, to set limits).</p>` },
    { s: "2. The rules of definition (B)", b: `<p>Six rules. A definition should be:</p><ol><li><b>Coextensive</b>: neither too broad nor too narrow (the most important rule, and the hardest)</li><li><b>Clear</b>, not obscure</li><li><b>Literal</b>, not metaphorical</li><li><b>Brief</b>, not long</li><li><b>Positive</b>, not negative, if possible</li><li><b>Not circular</b></li></ol><p>Condensed: coextensive; clear, literal and brief; not negative or circular.</p>` },
    { s: "3. The kinds of definition", b: `<p>I. <b>Nominal</b>: defines the word, not the thing (conventional, stipulative, synonym, etymology, examples).</p><p>II. <b>Real</b>:</p><ul><li>A. <b>Essential</b>: genus + specific difference ("rational animal")</li><li>B. Non-essential: by <b>properties</b> ("the animal that speaks"), by <b>accidents</b>, by <b>causes</b> (efficient, final, or material), or by <b>effects</b></li></ul><p>Use the <b>proximate</b> (narrowest) genus: "animal," not "organism," for man.</p>` },
    { s: "4. The limits of definition", b: `<p>Five things cannot be properly defined:</p><ol><li>The <b>infinite</b> (so God cannot be defined)</li><li><b>Individuals</b> (only species have specific differences)</li><li>The <b>summa genera</b> (the categories), which have no higher genus</li><li><b>Being</b> itself</li><li>The <b>transcendentals</b>, the properties of all being</li></ol><p>We can still say true things about them and distinguish them from other things.</p>` },
  ],
  5: [
    { s: "1. Judgments, propositions, and sentences", b: `<p>A <b>proposition</b> is a declarative sentence, the logical expression of a judgment; it alone is true or false. Questions, commands, exclamations and performatives ("I dub thee knight") are not propositions.</p><p>Propositions are <b>simple (categorical)</b>, with one subject and one predicate, or <b>compound</b>: hypothetical (if…then), disjunctive (either…or), or conjunctive (both…and / not both).</p>` },
    { s: "2. What is truth? (P)", b: `<p>Defining truth is easy; finding it is harder. Aristotle defined it in words of one syllable: <i>to say of what is that it is, and of what is not that it is not, is true.</i> Truth is the mind's conformity to reality.</p>` },
    { s: "3. The four kinds of categorical propositions (B)", b: `<p>A proposition's <b>matter</b> is its two terms; its <b>form</b> is its <b>quantity</b> (universal or particular) and <b>quality</b> (affirmative or negative).</p><ul><li><b>A</b>: universal affirmative ("All men are mortal")</li><li><b>E</b>: universal negative ("No men are mortal")</li><li><b>I</b>: particular affirmative ("Some men are mortal")</li><li><b>O</b>: particular negative ("Some men are not mortal")</li></ul><p>From Latin <i>AffIrmo</i> and <i>nEgO</i>. <b>Singular</b> propositions ("Socrates was bald") are treated as universal.</p>` },
    { s: "4. Logical form (B)", b: `<p>Every proposition must fit one of four molds, with the matter inside square brackets:</p><ul><li>All [S] is [P]</li><li>No [S] is [P]</li><li>Some [S] is [P]</li><li>Some [S] is not [P]</li></ul><p>The <b>quantifier</b> (All, No, Some) gives the quantity; the <b>copula</b> ("is" / "is not") gives the quality. No other verb can be a copula, so "Birds fly" becomes All [birds] are [things that fly]. Kreeft writes "tw" for "that which": All [charity] is [tw begins at home].</p><p>Beware the "very tricky O": "All that glitters is not gold" means Some [tw glitters] is not [gold].</p>` },
    { s: "5–6. Euler's circles & tricky propositions", b: `<p><b>Euler's circles</b> picture S and P as circles: A puts S inside P; E keeps them apart; I and O overlap them.</p><ul><li><b>Exclusive</b> ("Only S is P") becomes All [P] is [S] (or No [non-S] is [P]). But "<b>The only</b> S is P" means All [S] is [P].</li><li><b>Exceptive</b> ("All except S are P") says two things.</li><li><b>Indesignate</b> (no quantifier): universal if the predicate belongs to the subject by nature ("Cliffs are dangerous"), particular if by accident ("Fans swarmed the field").</li><li>"A few" gives an I; "few" is usually an O. "Never" gives an E; "always" gives an A.</li></ul>` },
    { s: "7. The distribution of terms", b: `<p>A term is <b>distributed</b> if the proposition claims to know about <i>all</i> of its class.</p><table class="tbl"><tr><th></th><th>Subject</th><th>Predicate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>D</td><td>U</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>D</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>U</td><td>U</td></tr><tr><td>O</td><td>U</td><td>D</td></tr></table><p>The quantifier tells the subject's distribution; the quality tells the predicate's: <b>affirmative predicates are undistributed, negative predicates are distributed.</b> The E claims the most knowledge; the I claims the least.</p>` },
  ],
  6: [
    { s: "1. Immediate inference", b: `<p>An <b>immediate inference</b> moves from one proposition to another with only two terms and no middle term: conversion, obversion, contraposition (and opposition, in the next chapter).</p>` },
    { s: "2. Conversion (B)", b: `<p>To <b>convert</b>, interchange subject and predicate. Valid conversions: <b>E → E</b>, <b>I → I</b>, <b>A → I</b>, <b>E → O</b>.</p><p>An A cannot convert simply ("All men are mortal" does not give "All mortals are men"), and an <b>O cannot be converted at all</b>. Both would distribute in the conclusion a term undistributed in the premise.</p><p>Put predicates into noun form first: No [sheep] are [things that can swim] becomes No [things that can swim] are [sheep].</p>` },
    { s: "3. Obversion (B)", b: `<p>To <b>obvert</b>: (1) change the quality of the proposition, and (2) negate the predicate. Two negatives cancel.</p><ul><li>A → E: "All men are mortal" becomes "No men are immortal."</li><li>E → A: "No man is an island" becomes "All men are non-islands."</li><li>I → O; O → I</li></ul><p>Negate the <i>whole</i> predicate.</p>` },
    { s: "4. Contraposition (and inversion)", b: `<p>Contraposition = obvert, then convert (<b>partial</b> contrapositive), then obvert again (<b>full</b> contrapositive).</p><ul><li>A: All S is P becomes All non-P is non-S</li><li>E: No S is P becomes Some non-P is not non-S</li><li>I: no contrapositive (you would have to convert an O)</li><li>O: Some S is not P becomes Some non-P is not non-S</li></ul><p><b>Inversion</b> = convert, then obvert (partial), then convert (full). No new rules: it's all conversion and obversion.</p>` },
  ],
  7: [
    { s: "1. What is contradiction? (B)", b: `<p>Two propositions <b>contradict</b> when they cannot both be true and cannot both be false. This happens only when they (a) have the same subject and predicate and (b) differ in <b>both quantity and quality</b>: <b>A–O</b> and <b>E–I</b>.</p><p>Proverbs like "Out of sight, out of mind" and "Absence makes the heart grow fonder" don't contradict each other; they aren't really universal.</p>` },
    { s: "2. The Square of Opposition (B)", b: `<p><b>Opposition</b> is the relation between propositions with the same subject and predicate that differ in quality, quantity, or both.</p><ul><li><b>Contradictories</b> (A–O, E–I): one true, one false</li><li><b>Contraries</b> (A–E): can't both be true; can both be false</li><li><b>Subcontraries</b> (I–O): can't both be false; can both be true</li><li><b>Subalternation</b> (A–I, E–O): truth flows down from universal to particular; falsity flows up from particular to universal</li></ul><p>Three kinds of exercise: give the contradictory; judge an inference valid or invalid; or, given one proposition true or false, say whether another is true, false, or <b>unknown</b>.</p>` },
    { s: "3. Existential import (P)", b: `<p>Modern logic texts say universal propositions lack existential import and particular ones have it. Kreeft calls this arbitrary: "All witches are dangerous" and "Some witches are dangerous" assert the existence of witches equally (that is, not at all).</p><p>His simplest view: ordinary propositions don't assert existence; only explicitly existential propositions ("Unicorns are not real") do. That is why the traditional Square holds.</p>` },
    { s: "5. Practical uses of the Square", b: `<p>To refute a proposition, prove its <b>contradictory</b>. To refute "All lawyers are crooks," you need only "Some lawyers are not crooks," not "No lawyers are crooks" (the contrary, which is much harder to prove).</p>` },
  ],
  8: [
    { s: "1. What does \"reason\" mean? (P)", b: `<p>Man is the "rational animal." The older, larger meaning of "reason" includes understanding, wisdom, intuition, self-knowledge, conscience, and the appreciation of beauty, not just calculating. Our reasoning is indirect: it depends on prior sense experience and prior knowledge.</p>` },
    { s: "2. Foundations of the syllogism (P)", b: `<p>The syllogism rests on self-evident principles:</p><ul><li><i>Dictum de omni</i>: what is true of all of a class is true of each member.</li><li><i>Dictum de nullo</i>: what is denied of all is denied of each.</li><li>The <b>Law of Identity</b>: a thing is what it is.</li><li>The <b>Law of Non-Contradiction</b>: x is not non-x.</li><li>The <b>Law of Excluded Middle</b>: a thing is either x or not x.</li></ul>` },
    { s: "3. How to detect arguments", b: `<p>Like crabbing: (1) detect the argument, (2) get it into the net of logical form, (3) judge whether it is valid.</p><p><b>Conclusion indicators</b>: therefore, hence, so, thus, consequently, it follows that, which shows that…</p><p><b>Premise indicators</b>: because, since, for, as shown by…</p>` },
    { s: "4. Arguments vs. explanations", b: `<p>An <b>argument</b> tries to prove something in question: "He must be guilty <i>because</i> he has shifty eyes." An <b>explanation</b> gives the cause of something already known: "He is absent <i>because</i> he is ill."</p>` },
    { s: "5. Truth and validity", b: `<p><b>Truth</b> is a relation between a proposition and reality. <b>Validity</b> is a relation between premises and conclusion. Only arguments can be fallacious; an error of fact is not a fallacy.</p><ul><li>True premises + valid logic = true conclusion (guaranteed).</li><li>A valid argument with a false conclusion must have a false premise.</li><li>Any other combination leaves the conclusion uncertain.</li></ul>` },
  ],
  9: [
    { s: "1. Three meanings of \"because\"", b: `<ul><li><b>Physical</b>: cause and effect ("I will die because of cancer")</li><li><b>Logical</b>: premise and conclusion ("I will die because all men die and I am a man")</li><li><b>Psychological</b>: motive and act ("He believes in Heaven because he's afraid to die")</li></ul>` },
    { s: "2. The four causes (P)", b: `<p>Intrinsic causes:</p><ul><li><b>Formal</b>: what it is, its essence (an acorn is the seed of an oak)</li><li><b>Material</b>: what it is made of (pulp and shell)</li></ul><p>Extrinsic causes:</p><ul><li><b>Efficient</b>: the agent that makes it (the oak tree)</li><li><b>Final</b>: its purpose (to become an oak)</li></ul><p>Any cause can be used as an <b>argument</b> (to prove something controversial) or an <b>explanation</b> (to account for something known).</p>` },
    { s: "3. A classification of arguments", b: `<ul><li>By <b>form</b>: immediate inference (one premise, two terms) vs. mediate (two or more premises, three or more terms). Mediate arguments are deductive (simple or compound syllogisms) or inductive.</li><li>By <b>causality</b>: cause to effect, or effect to cause (formal, material, efficient, final)</li><li>By <b>direction</b>: premise to conclusion, or conclusion back to premise</li><li>By <b>length</b>: one-step or multiple (chains)</li><li>By <b>strategy</b>: linear (one river with tributaries; Socrates' way) or cumulative (many rivers into one lake)</li></ul>` },
    { s: "4–6. Maps, deduction and induction, Socratic method", b: `<p>Simple <b>argument maps</b> draw an arrow from premises (feathered end) to conclusion (point).</p><p><b>Deduction</b> claims certainty; <b>induction</b> claims only probability.</p><p><b>Socratic method</b> combines them: induction gathers examples to reach a definition, and deduction tests the definition's consequences.</p>` },
  ],
  10: [
    { s: "1. Structure of the syllogism (B)", b: `<p>A syllogism has <b>three propositions</b> (two premises, one conclusion) and <b>three terms</b>, each used twice.</p><ul><li><b>Minor term (S)</b>: subject of the conclusion</li><li><b>Major term (P)</b>: predicate of the conclusion</li><li><b>Middle term (M)</b>: in both premises, not in the conclusion</li><li><b>Major premise</b>: contains P; <b>minor premise</b>: contains S</li></ul><p>To analyze: (1) find the conclusion, (2) find S and P, (3) find the premises, (4) the term left over is M.</p>` },
    { s: "2–3. The skeptic's and empiricist's objections (P)", b: `<p>The <b>skeptic</b> objects that every premise must itself be proved by another syllogism, and so on forever: an infinite regress. Aristotle's answer: there is no infinite regress, because the first principles of reasoning are <b>self-evident</b> and need no proof.</p><p>The <b>empiricist</b> (Hume, Mill) objects that the syllogism begs the question: you can't know "All men are mortal" unless you already know Socrates is mortal. Kreeft answers that we learn not only by experiencing particulars but by <b>understanding universals</b>: we abstract the essence (the term's <b>comprehension</b>) and see what necessarily follows from it.</p>` },
    { s: "4–5. Demonstrative and convincing syllogisms", b: `<p>A <b>demonstrative</b> syllogism proves its conclusion from premises that are certain and necessary, ideally through the essence (formal cause).</p><p>To build a <b>convincing</b> syllogism: start from the conclusion you want, find a middle term that links S and P, and choose premises your opponent already accepts.</p>` },
  ],
  11: [
    { s: "1. Euler's Circles (B)", b: `<p>Draw each premise as circles. If the premises force the conclusion's picture, the syllogism is valid; if the premises can be drawn without the conclusion being true, it is invalid.</p>` },
    { s: "2. Aristotle's six rules (B)", b: `<ol><li>Exactly <b>three terms</b>. Violation: Fallacy of <b>Four Terms</b>.</li><li>Exactly <b>three propositions</b>.</li><li>The <b>middle term must be distributed at least once</b>. Violation: <b>Undistributed Middle</b>.</li><li>No term undistributed in a premise may be distributed in the conclusion. Violation: <b>Illicit Major</b> or <b>Illicit Minor</b>.</li><li>No syllogism can have <b>two negative premises</b>.</li><li>If one premise is negative, the conclusion must be negative, and vice versa.</li></ol><p><b>Corollaries</b>: no two particular premises; a particular premise requires a particular conclusion. "The conclusion always follows the weaker premise."</p>` },
    { s: "3. \"Barbara Celarent\": mood and figure", b: `<p><b>Mood</b>: the letters (A, E, I, O) of the major premise, minor premise, and conclusion, in that order.</p><p><b>Figure</b>: where the middle term is placed.</p><ol><li>M–P, S–M</li><li>P–M, S–M</li><li>M–P, M–S</li><li>P–M, M–S</li></ol><p>The medieval mnemonic, where the vowels give the mood: <i>Barbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio</i> · <i>Camestres, Cesare, Baroko, Festino</i> · <i>Darapti, Disamis, Datisi, Felapton, Bokardo, Ferison</i> · <i>Bramantip, Camenes, Dimaris, Fesapo, Fresison</i>.</p>` },
    { s: "4. Venn Diagrams", b: `<p>Three overlapping circles for S, P, and M. Shade empty areas for universal premises and mark an X for particular ones. If the conclusion is already shown, the syllogism is valid.</p>` },
  ],
  12: [
    { s: "1. Enthymemes (B)", b: `<p>An <b>enthymeme</b> is a syllogism with one proposition "kept in mind" (<i>en-thymos</i>). It is the most common form of argument.</p><ul><li><b>First order</b>: missing major premise</li><li><b>Second order</b>: missing minor premise</li><li><b>Third order</b>: missing conclusion ("draw your own conclusion")</li></ul><p>Steps: (1) recognize it, (2) supply the missing premise, (3) test for validity. Smoking out hidden assumptions is one of the most practical skills in logic.</p>` },
    { s: "2. Sorites: chain syllogisms", b: `<p>A <b>sorites</b> is a chain of syllogisms in which the conclusion of each becomes a premise of the next, with the intermediate conclusions left unstated. ("All A are B, all B are C, all C are D, therefore all A are D.")</p>` },
    { s: "3–4. Epicheiremas & complex argument maps", b: `<p>An <b>epicheirema</b> is a syllogism in which at least one premise is backed by its own reason ("…because…"): a syllogism with an enthymeme attached.</p><p><b>Complex argument maps</b> chart these multi-step arguments with arrows from premises to conclusions.</p>` },
  ],
  13: [
    { s: "1. Hypothetical syllogisms (B)", b: `<p>"If p then q" (p ⊃ q): p is the <b>antecedent</b>, q the <b>consequent</b>. In a <i>pure</i> hypothetical syllogism all three propositions are hypothetical. In a <i>mixed</i> one, only the first is.</p><ul><li><b>Affirming the Antecedent</b> (p ⊃ q; p; ∴ q): valid</li><li><b>Denying the Consequent</b> (p ⊃ q; ~q; ∴ ~p): valid</li><li><b>Affirming the Consequent</b>: invalid</li><li><b>Denying the Antecedent</b>: invalid</li></ul><p>The reason: more than one cause can produce the same effect.</p>` },
    { s: "2–3. Reductio & the practical syllogism", b: `<p><b>Reductio ad absurdum</b> is Denying the Consequent when the consequent is absurd: "if your idea were true, this absurdity would follow; it doesn't; so your idea is false." It is Socrates' favorite weapon. Its form is valid, so to answer it you must deny a premise (usually that p really implies q).</p><p>The <b>practical syllogism</b> argues about means and ends: "If I am to get to England by tomorrow, I must buy a ticket today. I want to. So I must buy a ticket today."</p>` },
    { s: "4–5. Disjunctive & conjunctive syllogisms (B)", b: `<p><b>Disjunctive</b> ("either p or q"): <b>denying</b> one alternative is valid (p ∨ q; ~p; ∴ q). <b>Affirming</b> one alternative is invalid in a <b>weak</b> disjunction (at least one, maybe both), valid only in a <b>strong</b> one ("either… or…, but not both").</p><p><b>Conjunctive</b> ("not both p and q"): affirming one is valid (~(p & q); p; ∴ ~q). Denying one is invalid (~(p & q); ~p; ∴ q).</p>` },
    { s: "6. Dilemmas (B)", b: `<p>Two "horns," each leading to the same conclusion. Four forms, all valid: <b>Simple Constructive</b>, <b>Complex Constructive</b>, <b>Simple Destructive</b>, <b>Complex Destructive</b>. They are named by their conclusion: simple (categorical) or complex (disjunctive), constructive (affirmative) or destructive (negative).</p><p><b>Three ways to answer a dilemma:</b></p><ul><li><b>Taking it by the horns</b>: deny the hypothetical premise</li><li><b>Escaping between the horns</b>: deny the disjunctive premise (find a third option)</li><li><b>Rebuttal</b>: build a counter-dilemma proving the opposite (it leaves the original untouched)</li></ul>` },
  ],
  14: [
    { s: "1. What is induction?", b: `<p>Six kinds: generalization, causal arguments, scientific hypotheses, statistical probability, analogy, and <i>a fortiori</i>/<i>a minore</i>.</p><p>"From particular to universal" is not quite right. The best definition: an inductive argument claims to prove its conclusion only with <b>probability</b>, not certainty. Its strength comes from its matter, not its form.</p>` },
    { s: "2. Generalization", b: `<p>"Induction by simple enumeration": this swan is white, and that one…, so probably all swans are white. It is stronger as examples are more numerous, varied, and representative. Only a <b>complete induction</b> (every case examined) is certain.</p>` },
    { s: "3. Causal arguments: Mill's methods", b: `<ol><li><b>Agreement</b>: the one circumstance all instances share is the cause.</li><li><b>Difference</b>: the one circumstance in which an occurring and a non-occurring instance differ is the cause.</li><li><b>Joint Method</b> of agreement and difference</li><li><b>Residues</b>: subtract known causes of known effects; the remaining cause explains the remaining effect.</li><li><b>Concomitant Variations</b>: what varies whenever another thing varies is causally connected to it.</li></ol>` },
    { s: "4–7. Hypotheses, probability, analogy, a fortiori", b: `<p><b>Scientific hypotheses</b>: a problem arises, a preliminary hypothesis is proposed, its consequences are deduced and tested.</p><p><b>Statistical probability</b> can be exact ("a 5% margin of error").</p><p><b>Analogy</b>: two things alike in some ways are probably alike in another. It is stronger when the resemblances are real and relevant.</p><p><b>A fortiori</b> ("all the stronger"): if true here, all the more true there. <b>A minore</b> ("all the less") is its negative form.</p>` },
  ],
  15: [
    { s: "1. How to write a logical essay", b: `<ol><li>Choose a good topic: controversial, specific, and either/or.</li><li>Explain why the question is important.</li><li>Give your answer (thesis), up front.</li><li>Define your terms (if ambiguous, obscure, or controversial).</li><li>Prove your thesis with arguments.</li><li>Summarize and answer your opponent's arguments.</li><li>Anticipate and answer objections.</li></ol>` },
    { s: "2–3. Socratic dialogue & debate", b: `<p>Writing a dialogue: begin, imitate Plato, and use two characters, Socrates and "O." Socrates asks rather than tells, with ironic contrast; his goal is ultimately moral, and he aims to help, not harm.</p><p>Debate rules: define the question early; formulate it <b>disjunctively</b> (only two answers); agree on definitions of ambiguous terms; end in proof or in honest aporia.</p>` },
    { s: "4. Socratic method on difficult people", b: `<p>Establish the Socratic relationship. Get clear on the other person's thesis, understand it as they do (their definitions), and find their reasons. Show you are on their side, then explore the consequences together, using disjunctive options and dilemmas.</p>` },
    { s: "5. How to read a book Socratically", b: `<p>Distinguish <b>data, interpretation, and criticism</b>. Read actively, and ask the book six questions:</p><ol><li><b>Classification</b>: what kind of book is it?</li><li><b>Summary</b>: what is it about?</li><li><b>Outline</b>: how do the parts relate?</li><li><b>Argument</b>: what are its terms, propositions, arguments?</li><li><b>Evaluation</b>: is it true?</li><li><b>Application</b>: what difference does it make?</li></ol>` },
  ],
  16: [
    { s: "Logic and the divisions of philosophy (P)", b: `<p>Logic is not philosophy, but an instrument for it, as a telescope is to astronomy. Kreeft samples how logic touches each branch of philosophy and its basic question:</p><ul><li><b>Theology</b>: God, the Ultimate Reality</li><li><b>Metaphysics</b>: reality as such</li><li><b>Cosmology</b>: the visible universe</li><li><b>Philosophical anthropology</b>: human nature</li><li><b>Epistemology</b>: how we know</li><li><b>Ethics</b>: good and evil, the good life and the good society</li></ul><p>In each, the tools of the book (clear terms, true premises, valid arguments, distinctions, dilemmas, reductios) sharpen the great questions.</p>` },
  ],
};

// ---------- TERMS & DEFINITIONS (flashcards, quiz, matching) ----------
window.TERMS = [
  // Introduction
  [0, "Logic", "The science and art of reasoning well; it studies the forms or structures of thought."],
  [0, "Premises", "The reasons or evidence offered for the truth of the conclusion."],
  [0, "Conclusion", "What you are trying to prove to be true."],
  [0, "Inductive reasoning", "Reasons from particular premises, usually to a more general conclusion; yields only probability."],
  [0, "Deductive reasoning", "Reasons from at least one universal premise, usually to a more particular conclusion; when correct, yields certainty."],
  [0, "The three checkpoints of a deductive argument", "(1) All terms clear and unambiguous, (2) all premises true, (3) the argument logically valid."],
  [0, "Valid argument", "One whose conclusion necessarily follows from its premises: if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true."],
  [0, "The three acts of the mind", "Simple apprehension (understanding), judging, and reasoning."],
  [0, "Simple apprehension", "The first act of the mind: conceiving or understanding one object of thought; its product is a concept, expressed as a term."],
  [0, "Judging", "The second act of the mind: relating two concepts by predicating one of the other; expressed as a proposition."],
  [0, "Reasoning", "The third act of the mind: moving from two or more judgments (premises) to another (the conclusion); expressed as an argument."],
  [0, "Organon", "\"Instrument\": the name for Aristotle's six logical works, the world's first logic textbook."],
  [0, "Symbolic (mathematical) logic", "The modern logic of Russell and Whitehead's Principia Mathematica (1913); efficient like shorthand, but far from ordinary language."],

  // I. Understanding
  [1, "Understanding", "The power that distinguishes man from beasts and computers, shown above all in asking questions."],
  [1, "Concept", "The product of the first act of the mind; abstract, universal, necessary in its relations, and unchanging."],
  [1, "Universal", "\"One with respect to many\": one meaning or nature predicable of many things."],
  [1, "Term", "Any word or group of words that denotes one object of thought and can be the subject or predicate of a proposition."],
  [1, "Word", "The physical, conventional, changeable linguistic expression of a term in a particular language."],
  [1, "Extreme Realism", "Plato's view that universals (Forms) are real things, existing in a world of their own."],
  [1, "Nominalism", "The view (credited to Ockham) that universals are only names we use as shorthand; Kreeft finds it self-contradictory."],
  [1, "Moderate Realism", "Aristotle's (and Aquinas's) view: essences are objectively real but exist universally only in the mind, by abstraction from individual things."],
  [1, "Extension", "All the real things a term refers to: its \"population.\" Also called denotation or reference."],
  [1, "Comprehension", "A term's inner meaning (e.g. man = rational animal). Also called intension, connotation, or meaning."],
  [1, "Principle of Inverse Relation", "As a term's comprehension increases, its extension decreases, and vice versa."],

  // II. Terms
  [2, "Ambiguous term", "A term used with more than one meaning."],
  [2, "Vague term", "An inexact term (\"tall\"), which is not necessarily ambiguous or unclear."],
  [2, "Univocal", "Used with one and only one meaning."],
  [2, "Equivocal", "Used with two or more quite different and unrelated meanings (river bank / money bank)."],
  [2, "Analogical", "Used with meanings partly the same and partly different, and related to each other (a good man, a good dog, a good meal)."],
  [2, "Metaphorical term", "A term not used literally (Herod a \"fox\"); a metaphor is literally false, an analogy is not."],
  [2, "Syncategorematic term", "A word like \"the,\" \"on,\" or \"when\" that cannot stand alone as a subject or predicate."],
  [2, "Universal / particular / singular term", "Designates all members of a class / some members / only one individual."],
  [2, "Collective vs. divisive", "A term used for the group as a whole vs. for each individual member."],
  [2, "Relative term", "A term that cannot be thought without reference to something else (\"father,\" \"winner\")."],
  [2, "Categories", "Aristotle's ten highest classes (summa genera): substance, quantity, quality, relation, place, time, posture, possession, action, passion."],
  [2, "Substance", "An individual thing or entity, existing in itself; the first category."],
  [2, "Passion (category)", "Being acted upon (e.g. being cut or burned)."],
  [2, "Predicables", "The five possible relations of a predicate to its subject: species, genus, specific difference, property, accident."],
  [2, "Species (predicable)", "States the whole essence of the subject (\"Man is a rational animal\")."],
  [2, "Genus", "States the general or common aspect of the subject's essence (\"animal\")."],
  [2, "Specific difference", "States the aspect of the essence that differentiates the subject from other members of its genus (\"rational\")."],
  [2, "Property (predicable)", "Not the essence itself but flowing from it, and so always present (\"able to laugh,\" \"mortal\")."],
  [2, "Accident (predicable)", "A characteristic that is not essential and can come and go (\"bald,\" \"Athenian\")."],
  [2, "Tree of Porphyry", "A diagram dividing Being into substance and accidents, then down through genera to man, showing the inverse relation of extension and comprehension."],
  [2, "Rules of division", "A division must be exclusive, exhaustive, and use only one standard."],
  [2, "Dichotomous division", "Division into two classes, one the negation of the other (loving / non-loving)."],

  // III. Material Fallacies
  [3, "Material fallacy", "A mistake in the meaning or use of terms (content), as distinct from a formal fallacy (a mistake in reasoning)."],
  [3, "Equivocation", "Using the same term in two or more senses in the course of an argument."],
  [3, "Amphiboly", "Ambiguous syntax (word order or grammatical structure), not an ambiguous word."],
  [3, "Accent", "Ambiguity from voice inflection, ironic tone, facial expression, or innuendo."],
  [3, "Slanting", "The \"question-begging epithet\": a word that both describes and evaluates at once."],
  [3, "Straw Man", "Refuting an unfairly weak or ridiculous version of the opponent's idea instead of what he actually holds."],
  [3, "Ad hominem", "An argument addressed to the person instead of the issue: a personal attack."],
  [3, "Poisoning the Well", "Directly attacking the trustworthiness of the speaker instead of addressing his statement."],
  [3, "Ad verecundiam", "The appeal to reverence: an illegitimate appeal to authority, or an appeal to illegitimate authority."],
  [3, "Ad baculum", "The appeal to force (\"baculum\" = stick), i.e. to fear instead of reason."],
  [3, "Ad misericordiam", "The appeal to pity as a substitute for argument."],
  [3, "Ad ignominiam", "The appeal to shame as a substitute for a reason."],
  [3, "Ad populum", "Believing or doing something only because it is popular; the appeal to the masses."],
  [3, "Ad ignorantiam", "Arguing that an idea must be true because we do not know that it is not."],
  [3, "Dicto simpliciter", "Applying a general principle to a special case without the needed qualification."],
  [3, "Special Case", "The reverse of dicto simpliciter: arguing that what is true in a special case is true simply."],
  [3, "Composition", "Arguing from part to whole, ignoring that what is true of the part need not be true of the whole."],
  [3, "Division (fallacy)", "Arguing from whole to part, ignoring that what is true of the whole need not be true of the part."],
  [3, "Black-and-White Fallacy", "Allowing no gradations between extremes: \"it is not this extreme, so it must be the opposite.\""],
  [3, "Non sequitur", "\"It does not follow\": the master fallacy of argumentation."],
  [3, "Ignoratio elenchi", "\"Ignorance of the chain\": giving reasons that prove a different conclusion; missing the point."],
  [3, "Begging the question (petitio principii)", "Assuming what you set out to prove, smuggling the conclusion into the premises."],
  [3, "Complex Question", "A question that cannot be answered without \"begging\" another question."],
  [3, "Arguing in a Circle", "Using a conclusion to justify a premise after using that premise to justify the conclusion."],
  [3, "Hasty Generalization", "Inferring a general principle from too few or unrepresentative examples."],
  [3, "Post hoc ergo propter hoc", "\"After this, therefore because of this\": inferring a cause merely from temporal sequence."],
  [3, "Hypothesis Contrary to Fact", "The \"if only\" fallacy: claiming to know what would have happened if something not true were true."],
  [3, "Argument from Silence", "Concluding that an author doesn't believe in x, or that there is no x, because he is silent about it."],
  [3, "Selective Evidence", "Citing only the evidence for your hypothesis and ignoring the evidence against it."],
  [3, "Shifting the Burden of Proof", "Demanding that the other side prove its case when the burden belongs to you."],
  [3, "Reductionism (\"Nothing Buttery\")", "Claiming a thing is \"nothing but\" something lower (love is nothing but lust)."],
  [3, "Fallacy of Accident", "Confusing the accidental with the essential (or the essential with the accidental)."],
  [3, "Misplaced Concreteness", "Treating an abstraction as if it were a concrete thing (Whitehead's phrase)."],
  [3, "Existential Fallacy", "Confusing essence and existence."],

  // IV. Definition
  [4, "Definition", "An expression telling what a thing is; it sets limits around a thing (de-finio)."],
  [4, "Essential definition", "The ideal definition: genus plus specific difference."],
  [4, "Proximate genus", "The narrowest genus: \"animal,\" not \"organism,\" for man."],
  [4, "The six rules of definition", "Coextensive (neither too broad nor too narrow), clear, literal, brief, positive if possible, and not circular."],
  [4, "Too broad", "The definition includes things the term does not (man as \"two-legged animal\")."],
  [4, "Too narrow", "The definition leaves out things the term includes (man as \"male rational animal\")."],
  [4, "Circular definition", "The term defined appears in the definition."],
  [4, "Nominal definition", "A definition of the word rather than the thing (conventional, stipulative, synonym, etymology, or examples)."],
  [4, "Stipulative definition", "A nominal definition proposing a special meaning; neither true nor false."],
  [4, "Real definition", "A definition of the thing itself: essential, or by properties, accidents, causes, or effects."],
  [4, "Definition by final cause", "Defining by purpose: \"An eye is an organ for seeing.\""],
  [4, "Definition by efficient cause", "Defining by the agent that produces it (a \"genetic\" definition): \"An acorn is a seed produced by an oak.\""],
  [4, "Definition by material cause", "Defining by contents: \"Water is two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\""],
  [4, "Limits of definition", "The infinite, individuals, the summa genera, being itself, and the transcendentals cannot be properly defined."],

  // V. Judgment
  [5, "Proposition", "A declarative sentence: the logical expression of a judgment, which is either true or false."],
  [5, "Categorical proposition", "A simple proposition with one subject and one predicate."],
  [5, "Compound proposition", "Two or more simple propositions joined: hypothetical (if…then), disjunctive (either…or), or conjunctive (both…and / not both)."],
  [5, "Truth", "Saying of what is that it is, and of what is not that it is not; the mind's conformity to reality."],
  [5, "Quantity", "How much of the subject we are talking about: universal (all) or particular (some)."],
  [5, "Quality", "Whether the predicate is affirmed or denied of the subject: affirmative or negative."],
  [5, "A proposition", "Universal affirmative: All [S] is [P]."],
  [5, "E proposition", "Universal negative: No [S] is [P]."],
  [5, "I proposition", "Particular affirmative: Some [S] is [P]."],
  [5, "O proposition", "Particular negative: Some [S] is not [P]."],
  [5, "Singular proposition", "About one individual (\"Socrates was bald\"); treated as universal."],
  [5, "Quantifier", "The word before the subject in logical form: \"All,\" \"No\" (universal) or \"Some\" (particular)."],
  [5, "Copula", "\"Is\" or \"is not\" (\"are\" / \"are not\"): it indicates the quality. No other verb can be a copula."],
  [5, "\"tw\"", "Kreeft's abbreviation for \"that which\" in logical form: All [charity] is [tw begins at home]."],
  [5, "Exclusive proposition", "Begins with \"only\": \"Only S is P\" = All [P] is [S]. (But \"The only S is P\" = All [S] is [P].)"],
  [5, "Exceptive proposition", "Begins with \"all except\"; it really says two things and should become two propositions."],
  [5, "Indesignate proposition", "Has no quantifier; universal if the predicate belongs to the subject by nature, particular if by accident."],
  [5, "Distributed term", "A term the proposition claims to know about universally, i.e. all of its class."],
  [5, "Rule of distribution", "Universals distribute the subject; negatives distribute the predicate. A: S; E: S and P; I: neither; O: P."],
  [5, "Euler's circles", "Circle diagrams of S and P: A puts S inside P; E separates them; I and O overlap."],

  // VI. Changing Propositions
  [6, "Immediate inference", "Inference from one proposition to another, with only two terms (no middle term)."],
  [6, "Conversion", "Interchanging subject and predicate. Valid: E→E, I→I, A→I, E→O. An O cannot be converted."],
  [6, "Illicit conversion", "Converting an A simply (to an A) or converting an O; it distributes a term in the conclusion that was undistributed in the premise."],
  [6, "Obversion", "Changing the quality of the proposition and negating the predicate: A→E, E→A, I→O, O→I."],
  [6, "Contraposition", "Obvert, convert (partial contrapositive), then obvert again (full contrapositive). The I has no contrapositive."],
  [6, "Full contrapositive of A", "All [S] is [P] becomes All [non-P] is [non-S]."],
  [6, "Inversion", "Convert, then obvert (partial inverse), then convert again (full inverse)."],

  // VII. Contradiction
  [7, "Contradiction", "The relation between two propositions that cannot both be true and cannot both be false."],
  [7, "Conditions for contradiction", "Same subject and predicate, differing in both quantity and quality: A–O and E–I."],
  [7, "Opposition", "The relation between propositions with the same subject and predicate that differ in quality, quantity, or both."],
  [7, "Contraries", "A and E: cannot both be true, but can both be false."],
  [7, "Subcontraries", "I and O: cannot both be false, but can both be true."],
  [7, "Subalternation", "A–I and E–O: truth flows down from the universal to the particular; falsity flows up."],
  [7, "Existential import", "A proposition's claim that its subject exists. Kreeft holds that only explicitly existential propositions have it."],
  [7, "To refute a proposition", "Prove its contradictory (e.g. to refute \"All lawyers are crooks,\" prove \"Some lawyers are not crooks\")."],

  // VIII. Reasoning
  [8, "Dictum de omni", "\"The law about all\": what is true of every member of a class is true of each."],
  [8, "Dictum de nullo", "\"The law about none\": what is denied of all of a class is denied of each."],
  [8, "Law of Identity", "A thing is what it is."],
  [8, "Law of Non-Contradiction", "A thing is not what it isn't; x is not non-x."],
  [8, "Law of Excluded Middle", "A thing is either x or not x."],
  [8, "Conclusion indicators", "\"Therefore,\" \"hence,\" \"so,\" \"thus,\" \"consequently,\" \"it follows that\"…"],
  [8, "Premise indicators", "\"Because,\" \"since,\" \"for\"…"],
  [8, "Argument vs. explanation", "An argument proves something in question; an explanation gives the cause of something already known."],
  [8, "Fallacy", "A mistake in reasoning, which makes an argument invalid; an error of fact is not a fallacy."],
  [8, "Truth vs. validity", "Truth relates a proposition to reality; validity relates premises to conclusion."],

  // IX. Kinds of arguments
  [9, "Three meanings of \"because\"", "Physical (cause and effect), logical (premise and conclusion), and psychological (motive and act)."],
  [9, "Formal cause", "The essence of a thing, what it is; expressed in its definition."],
  [9, "Material cause", "What a thing is made of: its contents or raw material."],
  [9, "Efficient cause", "The agent that makes, moves, or changes the effect."],
  [9, "Final cause", "The purpose or end for the sake of which a thing is."],
  [9, "Mediate inference", "Inference with at least two premises and three terms (deductive or inductive)."],
  [9, "Linear vs. cumulative argument", "A linear argument is one chain to one conclusion (Socrates' way); a cumulative one uses many independent arguments for the same conclusion."],
  [9, "Argument map", "A diagram with an arrow from premises (feathered end) to conclusion (point)."],
  [9, "Socratic method", "Combining induction (examples leading to a definition) and deduction (testing its consequences) through questioning."],

  // X. Syllogisms
  [10, "Syllogism", "A deductive argument with three propositions (two premises and a conclusion) and three terms, each used twice."],
  [10, "Minor term (S)", "The subject of the conclusion."],
  [10, "Major term (P)", "The predicate of the conclusion."],
  [10, "Middle term (M)", "The term in each premise but not in the conclusion."],
  [10, "Major premise", "The premise containing the major term."],
  [10, "Minor premise", "The premise containing the minor term."],
  [10, "Skeptic's objection", "That every premise needs proof by another syllogism, an infinite regress. Answer: first principles are self-evident."],
  [10, "Empiricist's objection", "Hume and Mill: the syllogism begs the question, since the universal premise presupposes the conclusion. Answer: we understand essences, not just particulars."],
  [10, "Demonstrative syllogism", "A syllogism that proves its conclusion from certain, necessary premises."],

  // XI. Checking validity
  [11, "Rule 1", "A syllogism must have three and only three terms (violation: Fallacy of Four Terms)."],
  [11, "Rule 2", "A syllogism must have three and only three propositions."],
  [11, "Rule 3", "The middle term must be distributed at least once (violation: Undistributed Middle)."],
  [11, "Rule 4", "No term undistributed in a premise may be distributed in the conclusion (violation: Illicit Major or Illicit Minor)."],
  [11, "Rule 5", "No syllogism can have two negative premises."],
  [11, "Rule 6", "If one premise is negative, the conclusion must be negative; and if the conclusion is negative, one premise must be negative."],
  [11, "Corollaries", "No syllogism may have two particular premises; a particular premise requires a particular conclusion."],
  [11, "Fallacy of Four Terms", "A syllogism with four terms, usually because one term is used ambiguously."],
  [11, "Illicit Major", "The major term is distributed in the conclusion but undistributed in the major premise."],
  [11, "Illicit Minor", "The minor term is distributed in the conclusion but undistributed in the minor premise."],
  [11, "Mood", "The quality and quantity (A, E, I, O) of the major premise, minor premise, and conclusion, in that order."],
  [11, "Figure", "The placement of the middle term: 1st M–P/S–M; 2nd P–M/S–M; 3rd M–P/M–S; 4th P–M/M–S."],
  [11, "Barbara", "AAA in the first figure: the \"bull's eye\" syllogism."],
  [11, "Venn Diagrams", "Three overlapping circles for S, P, and M, shaded or marked to test validity."],

  // XII. More difficult syllogisms
  [12, "Enthymeme", "A syllogism with one proposition implied but not stated (\"kept in mind\")."],
  [12, "First / second / third order enthymeme", "Missing the major premise / the minor premise / the conclusion."],
  [12, "Sorites", "A chain of syllogisms in which intermediate conclusions are omitted."],
  [12, "Epicheirema", "A syllogism in which at least one premise is supported by its own reason (an attached enthymeme)."],

  // XIII. Compound syllogisms
  [13, "Antecedent", "The proposition following \"if\" in a hypothetical proposition."],
  [13, "Consequent", "The proposition following \"then\" in a hypothetical proposition."],
  [13, "Pure hypothetical syllogism", "All three propositions are hypothetical: if p then q; if q then r; therefore if p then r."],
  [13, "Mixed hypothetical syllogism", "Only the first premise is hypothetical; the second premise and conclusion are categorical."],
  [13, "Affirming the Antecedent", "p ⊃ q; p; therefore q. Valid."],
  [13, "Denying the Consequent", "p ⊃ q; ~q; therefore ~p. Valid."],
  [13, "Affirming the Consequent", "p ⊃ q; q; therefore p. Invalid."],
  [13, "Denying the Antecedent", "p ⊃ q; ~p; therefore ~q. Invalid."],
  [13, "Reductio ad absurdum", "Denying the Consequent when the consequent is absurd: if your idea were true, absurdity would follow."],
  [13, "Practical syllogism", "Reasoning about means and ends, usually in hypothetical form."],
  [13, "Weak vs. strong disjunction", "Weak: at least one, maybe both. Strong: one or the other but not both."],
  [13, "Disjunctive syllogism (valid form)", "Either p or q; not p; therefore q."],
  [13, "Conjunctive syllogism (valid form)", "Not both p and q; p; therefore not q."],
  [13, "Dilemma", "An argument whose double hypothetical premise sets up two \"horns,\" both leading to the same conclusion."],
  [13, "Taking a dilemma by the horns", "Denying its hypothetical premise."],
  [13, "Escaping between the horns", "Denying its disjunctive premise by finding a third alternative."],
  [13, "Rebuttal", "Constructing a counter-dilemma that proves the opposite conclusion; it leaves the original untouched."],

  // XIV. Induction
  [14, "Induction", "An argument that claims to prove its conclusion only with probability, not certainty."],
  [14, "Generalization (simple enumeration)", "Reasoning from particular instances to a probable universal conclusion."],
  [14, "Complete induction", "One in which every member of the class has been examined, so the conclusion is certain."],
  [14, "Method of Agreement", "Mill: the one circumstance common to all instances of a phenomenon is its cause (or effect)."],
  [14, "Method of Difference", "Mill: the one circumstance in which an instance with the phenomenon differs from one without it is the cause."],
  [14, "Joint Method", "Mill: using agreement and difference together."],
  [14, "Method of Residues", "Mill: subtract what is known to be caused by known antecedents; the residue is caused by the remaining antecedents."],
  [14, "Method of Concomitant Variations", "Mill: whatever varies whenever another phenomenon varies is causally connected to it."],
  [14, "Argument by analogy", "Reasoning that things alike in some respects are probably alike in another."],
  [14, "A fortiori", "\"All the stronger\": if true in one case, all the more true where the reason is stronger."],
  [14, "A minore", "\"All the less\": the negative version of the a fortiori argument."],

  // XV. Practical applications
  [15, "A good essay topic", "Controversial, specific, and an either/or question."],
  [15, "The seven steps of a logical essay", "Topic; its importance; thesis; define terms; prove it; answer opponents' arguments; answer objections."],
  [15, "Data, interpretation, criticism", "The three things to distinguish when reading a book: what it says, what it means, and whether it's true."],
  [15, "Six questions to ask a book", "Classification, summary, outline, argument, evaluation, application."],
  [15, "Socratic debate rule", "Formulate the question disjunctively, so it has only two possible answers, and define ambiguous terms by mutual agreement."],

  // XVI. Philosophical applications
  [16, "Logic and philosophy", "Logic is not philosophy but its instrument, as a telescope is to astronomy."],
  [16, "Divisions of philosophy", "Theology, metaphysics, cosmology, philosophical anthropology, epistemology, and ethics."],
  [16, "Epistemology", "The part of philosophy asking how we know."],
  [16, "Philosophical anthropology", "The part of philosophy asking about human nature."],
  [16, "Cosmology", "The part of philosophy asking about the visible universe."],
];

